"A395495 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A395495)\nciteturn0view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://oeis.org/A395495\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 142\nL0: 【0†login】\nL1: \nL2: The OEIS is supported by 【1†the many generous donors to the OEIS Foundation†oeisf.org】.\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Winners in the A400K Celebration】\nL5: \nL6: [Input]\nL7: \nL8: [Input]\nL9: \nL10: 【3†Hints】\nL11: \nL12: (Greetings from 【4†The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences】!)\nL13: \nL14: A395495\nL15: \nL16: Minimum number of cells in a connected polyomino on the square grid that contains every fixed n-omino as a translated subregion.\nL17: \nL18: 1\nL19: \nL20: 1, 3, 5, 9, 13, 18, 24, 31, 39\nL21: \nL22: (【5†list】; 【6†graph】; 【7†refs】; 【8†listen】; 【9†history】; 【10†text】; 【11†internal format】)\nL23: \nL24: OFFSET\nL25: 1,2\nL26: \nL27: COMMENTS\nL28: \nL29: Containment is translation-only: the contained piece may not be rotated or reflected. Number of fixed n-ominoes is 【12†A001168】. Each of a(1) through a(9) is proved via SAT + CEGAR connectivity and carries a DRAT UNSAT certificate at k = a(n) - 1 that is independently verified by drat-trim. A pure-Python geometric containment verifier also re-confirms that every reported container does contain every fixed n-omino as a translate.\nL30: Conjecture: a(n) = (n^2 - n + 6)/2 for n >= 4, with a(n) = 2n - 1 for n in {1, 2, 3}. Equivalently a(n) = 【13†A000217】(n-1) + 3 for n >= 4. Predicts a(10) = 48.\nL31: At n = 7 every minimum container has a single 1-cell interior hole. SAT model-enumeration over the 7 X 7 bounding box (the natural search box, which must contain both the horizontal and vertical n-cell I-omino) finds exactly four 24-cell containers; all four are the 25-cell discrete L_1 ball of radius 3 (centered in the 7 X 7 box) minus one cell from the D_4 orbit at L_1 distance 2 from the center, and all contain a hole. A wider 8 X 8 search at k = 23 returns UNSAT (LRAT certified).\nL32: The hole is therefore a structural feature of a(7), distinguishing this sequence geometrically from the purely numerical match 【14†A350660】. For n in {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9} the optimal container is simply connected.\nL33: LINKS\nL34: \nL35: 【15†Table of n, a(n) for n=1..9.】\nL36: \nL37: Peter Exley, 【16†Solver code, data, and figures†github.com】, GitHub.\nL38: \nL39: EXAMPLE\nL40: \nL41: Optimal containers, with one example per illustrated n. Cells are marked '#'; empty cells are '.'.\nL42: \nL43: The 1 container with bounding box 3 X 3:\nL44: \nL45: . # .\nL46: # # #\nL47: \nL48: . # .\nL49: \nL50: .\nL51: \nL52: The 1 container with bounding box 4 X 4:\nL53: \nL54: . . # .\nL55: \nL56: . # # .\nL57: \nL58: # # # #\nL59: \nL60: . # # .\nL61: \nL62: .\nL63: \nL64: The 1 container with bounding box 5 X 5:\nL65: \nL66: . . # . .\nL67: \nL68: . # # # .\nL69: \nL70: # # # # #\nL71: \nL72: . # # # .\nL73: \nL74: . . # . .\nL75: \nL76: .\nL77: \nL78: One of 4 D_4-equivalent containers with bounding box 7 X 7 (the single dot at row 3 column 3 is the interior hole):\nL79: \nL80: . . . # . . .\nL81: \nL82: . . # # # . .\nL83: \nL84: . # . # # # .\nL85: # # # # # # #\nL86: \nL87: . # # # # # .\nL88: \nL89: . . # # # . .\nL90: \nL91: . . . # . . .\nL92: \nL93: .\nL94: \nL95: The 1 container with bounding box 9 X 9:\nL96: \nL97: . . . . . # . . .\nL98: \nL99: . . . . # # . . .\nL100: \nL101: . . . # # # . . .\nL102: \nL103: . . # # # # # # #\nL104: \nL105: . # # # # # # # #\nL106: # # # # # # # # #\nL107: \nL108: . . . # # # . . .\nL109: \nL110: . . . # # # . . .\nL111: \nL112: . . . # # # . . .\nL113: \nL114: CROSSREFS\nL115: \nL116: Cf. 【17†A327094】 (the free-piece analog), 【12†A001168】 (number of fixed n-ominoes (the family contained)), 【18†A000105】 (number of free n-ominoes), 【13†A000217】.\nL117: \nL118: Sequence in context: 【19†A024403】 【20†A129230】 【21†A203567】 * 【14†A350660】 【22†A071404】 【23†A074133】\nL119: \nL120: Adjacent sequences: 【24†A395492】 【25†A395493】 【26†A395494】 * 【27†A395496】 【28†A395497】 【29†A395498】\nL121: \nL122: KEYWORD\nL123: \nL124: nonn,more\nL125: \nL126: AUTHOR\nL127: \nL128: 【30†Peter Exley】, Apr 26 2026\nL129: STATUS\nL130: \nL131: approved\nL132: \nL133: 【31†Lookup】 【32†Welcome】 【33†Wiki】 【34†Register】 【35†Music】 【36†Plot 2】 【37†Demos】 【38†Index】 【39†WebCam】 【40†Contribute】 【41†Format】 【42†Style Sheet】 【43†Transforms】 【44†Superseeker】 【45†Recents】\nL134: \nL135: 【46†The OEIS Community】\nL136: \nL137: Maintained by 【47†The OEIS Foundation Inc.†oeisf.org】\nL138: \nL139: Last modified October 29 05:35 EDT 2026. Contains 400785 sequences.\nL140: \nL141: 【48†License Agreements, Terms of Use, Privacy Policy】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn0view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://oeis.org/history?seq=A395495\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://oeis.org/history?seq=A395495 is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn0view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://oeis.org/history?seq=A395495&start=10\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://oeis.org/history?seq=A395495&start=10 is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nreference request - What is the smallest size of a shape in which all fixed $n$-polyominos can fit? - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/420897/what-is-the-smallest-size-of-a-shape-in-which-all-fixed-n-polyominos-can-fit)\nciteturn0view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://mathoverflow.net/questions/420897/what-is-the-smallest-size-of-a-shape-in-which-all-fixed-n-polyominos-can-fit\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 381\nL0: #### 184 communities for the curious\nL1: \nL2: MathOverflow is part of Stack Overflow’s open communities: specialist spaces where curiosity is welcome, knowledge is shared freely, and the best answers rise to the top.\nL3: \nL4: 【0†Find your community†stackexchange.com】\nL5: \nL6: [Input: Search on MathOverflow…]\nL7: \nL8:   1.   2.   3.   4.   5. 【1†Log in】\nL9:   6. 【2†Sign up】\nL10:   1.     1. 【3†Home】\nL11:     2. 【4†Questions】\nL12:     3. 【5†Unanswered】\nL13:     4. 【6†AI Assist†stackoverflow.com】\nL14:     5. 【7†Tags】\nL15:     6.     7. 【8†Chat†chat.stackexchange.com】\nL16:     8. 【9†Users】\nL17:     9. # 【10†What is the smallest size of a shape in which all fixed $n$-polyominos can fit?】\nL18: \nL19: 【11†Ask Question】\nL20: \nL21: Asked 4 years, 6 months ago\nL22: \nL23: Modified 【12†4 years, 5 months ago】\nL24: \nL25: Viewed 1k times\nL26: \nL27: [Input]\nL28: \nL29: 26\nL30: \nL31: [Input]\nL32: Let $n$ be an integer and consider all fixed $n$-polyominos, i.e., without rotation or reflection. I am interested in finding a shape in which all polyominos can embed. (It is OK if multiple polyominos overlap.)\nL33: \nL34: For instance, for $n=3$, the fixed 3-polyominos are:\nL35: \nL36:     `###  #..  ##.  ##.  #..  .#.\nL37:     ...  #..  #..  .#.  ##.  ##.\nL38:     ...  #..  ...  ...  ...  ...\nL39:     `\nL40: \nL41: and these polyominos all embed in the following shape with 5 cells, which is the best possible:\nL42: \nL43:     `.#.\nL44:     ###\nL45:     .#.\nL46:     `\nL47: More generally, a suitable shape for arbitrary $n$ is the $n \\times n$ square (with $n^2$ cells) and a naive lower bound would be $2n-1$ cells (necessary to embed the horizontal and vertical line $n$-polyomino).\nL48: \nL49: I define an integer sequence $S_n$ to be the minimal number of cells of a shape in which all $n$-polyominos embed, and I am interested in understanding this sequence. In particular, specific questions are:\nL50:   * Can we always find an optimal shape that fits into an $n \\times n$ square? (this seems intuitively reasonable but I do not know how to prove it)\nL51:   * Can we prove that, asymptotically, $S_n = \\Theta(n^2)$? (the challenge is to show an $\\Omega(n^2)$ lower bound -- maybe this is already possible by simply looking at a subset of the polyominos, but I couldn't see how to do it)\nL52: \nL53: More generally, has this sequence already been studied?\nL54: To understand what happens here, I was able to compute by bruteforce computer search the first values of $S_n$, making the assumption that optimal shapes always fit in an $n$ by $n$ square (first point above) -- these values may turn out not to be optimal if this assumption is wrong:\nL55: \nL56:   * We have $S_1 = 1$, $S_2 = 3$ (easily), and $S_3 = 5$ (see above)\nL57:   * We have $S_4=9$ with a surprising shape:\nL58: \nL59:     `..#.\nL60:     .##.\nL61:     ####\nL62:     .##.\nL63:     `\nL64:   * We have $S_5 = 13$, with the unsurprising shape:\nL65: \nL66:     `..#..\nL67:     .###.\nL68:     #####\nL69:     .###.\nL70:     ..#..\nL71:     `\nL72: \nL73:   * We have $S_6 = 18$ with a surprising shape:\nL74: \nL75:     `..##..\nL76:     ..##..\nL77:     ######\nL78:     #####.\nL79:     ..##..\nL80:     ..#...\nL81:     `\nL82: \nL83:   * We have $S_7 = 24$, the shape is similar to $n=5$ but with a hole:\nL84: \nL85:     `...#...\nL86:     ..###..\nL87:     .#.###.\nL88:     #######\nL89:     .#####.\nL90:     ..###..\nL91:     ...#...\nL92:     `\nL93: \nL94:   * I do not know $S_8$\nL95: There are matching sequences for these terms in OEIS, but their definitions do not seem relevant... Edit: maybe 【13†https://oeis.org/A203567†oeis.org】 【14†https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X01003570†www.sciencedirect.com】 would be worth investigating.\nL96: \nL97: Acknowledgement: This question is by 【15†Thomas Colcombet†www.irif.fr】 and 【16†Antonio Casares†www.labri.fr】.\nL98: \nL99: Edit: fixed the values of $S_5$ and $S_7$, many thanks to @RobPratt for noticing and reporting the errors!\nL100:   * 【17†reference-request】\nL101:   * 【18†co.combinatorics】\nL102:   * 【19†combinatorial-optimization】\nL103:   * 【20†integer-sequences】\nL104:   * 【21†extremal-combinatorics】\nL105: \nL106: [Button: Cite]\nL107: \nL108: 【22†Improve this question】\nL109: \nL110: Follow [Input]\nL111: \nL112: 【23†edited May 5, 2022 at 16:16】\nL113: \nL114: asked Apr 22, 2022 at 21:57\nL115: \nL116: 【24†a3nm】\nL117: \nL118: 592 3 3 silver badges 15 15 bronze badges\nL119:   * Seems like the \"straight polyominos\" (those obtained as a union of the squares touching a given segment, which need not be horizontal or vertical) should give the $\\Omega(n^2)$ lower bound, I'll write something tomorrow if no one has answered that part of the question\nL120: \nL121: –  【25†Saúl RM】\nL122: \nL123: Commented Apr 22, 2022 at 22:22\nL124: \nL125:   * You can at least get $S_n=\\Omega(n\\log n)$ by a simple argument based on the rectangles.\nL126: \nL127: –  【26†Steven Stadnicki】\nL128: \nL129: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 1:56\nL130: \nL131:   * 2\nL132: The case of the \"straight polyominos\" proposed by Saúl is the digital equivalent of the celebrated Kakeya problem: 【27†en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kakeya_set†en.wikipedia.org】\nL133: \nL134: –  【28†domotorp】\nL135: \nL136: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 5:13\nL137: \nL138:   * 2\nL139: @a3nm Take a nice set $S$ whose area is $<0.01n^2$ and contains a length $n$ segment in every direction. Such a set $S$ exists by the constructions given on Wikipeida for the Kakeya set. Now pixelize $S$ by checking which unit squares it intersects from the unit grid. The number of these squares is $0.01n^2 +O(n)$ as the perimeter of $S$ is nice. As $n\\to \\infty$, this gives an upper bound of $0.01n^2$, and we can pick $0.01$ as small as we want to.\nL140: \nL141: –  【28†domotorp】\nL142: \nL143: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 8:53\nL144: \nL145:   * 1\nL146: I've asked about something similar about free polyominoes on 【29†Code Golf Stack Exchange†codegolf.stackexchange.com】 and 【30†Math Stack Exchange†math.stackexchange.com】.\nL147: \nL148: –  【31†Peter Kagey】\nL149: \nL150: Commented Apr 24, 2022 at 2:11\nL151: \nL152:  |  【32†Show 17 more comments】\nL153: ## 3 Answers\nL154: \nL155: Sorted by:\nL156: \nL157: [Select]\nL158: \nL159: [Input]\nL160: \nL161: 9\nL162: \nL163: [Input]\nL164: It is actually $\\ge cn^2$ with some $c>0$. The value of $c$ I'll obtain is pretty dismal but I tried to trade the precision for the argument simplicity everywhere I could, so it can be certainly improved quite a bit. I have no doubt that it is written somewhere (perhaps, in the continuous form: the $2$-dimensional measure of a set containing a shift of every rectifiable curve of length $1$ is at least some positive constant) but I'll leave it to more educated people to provide the reference.\nL165: We shall work on the 2D $n\\times n$ lattice torus $T$ whose size $n$ is a power of $2$. Clearly, wrapping around makes the set only smaller. Define $K$ to be the integer such that $2^{K^3+K}\\le n< 2^{(K+1)^3+K+1}$ (I assume that $n$ is large enough, so $K$ is not too small either).\nL166: \nL167: Put $\\varepsilon_k=2^{-k}, M_k=2^{k^3}$ ($k\\ge 4$). Note that $\\frac 12+3\\sum_{k\\ge 4}\\varepsilon_k=\\frac 78<1$. Put $\\mu_k=\\frac 12+3\\sum_{m=4}^{k}\\varepsilon_m$ (so $\\mu_3=\\frac 12$).\nL168: Now take any set $E\\subset T$ of density $d(E,T)=\\frac{|E|}{|T|}=1/2$. Our aim will be to construct a connected set $P$ of cardinality $|P|\\le Cn$ such that no its lattice shift of $E$ on $T$ contains $P$.\nL169: Start with dividing $T$ into $M_4^2$ equal squares $Q_4$. Notice that the portion of squares $Q_4$ with density $d(E,Q_4)=\\frac{|E\\cap Q_4|}{|Q_4|}>\\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4$ is at most $\\mu_3/(\\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4)\\le 1-\\varepsilon_4$. Now choose $N_4=\\frac{2\\log_2 (M_4/\\varepsilon_4)}{\\varepsilon_4}=2\\cdot(4^3+4)\\cdot 2^4$ squares $Q_4$ independently at random.\nL170: The probability that none of them has density $d(E,Q_4)\\le \\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4$ is at most $(1-\\varepsilon_4)^{N_4}< \\left(\\frac{\\varepsilon_4}{M_4}\\right)^2$. This means that if we consider not only the standard partition but also all its shifts $E'$ by multiples of $\\varepsilon_4 n/M_4$, then there exists a configuration $P_4$ of $N_4$ squares $Q_4$ such that for each such shift, the density of $E'$ in at least one square $Q_4$ in $P_4$ is $d(E',Q_4)\\le \\mu_3+\\varepsilon_4$.\nL171: However, every lattice shift can be approximated by such shifts with precision $\\varepsilon_4 n/M_4=\\varepsilon_4\\ell(Q_4)$, so we conclude that for any shift $E'$ of $E$, the configuration $P_4$ contains a square $Q_4$ with density $d(E',Q_4)\\le\\mu_3+3\\varepsilon_4=\\mu_4$.\nL172: Our $P$ will be essentially contained in $\\bigcup_{Q_4\\in P_4}Q_4$. Notice that we can construct some set in each square $Q_4$ and the cost of joining them afterwords will be at most $ 2n N_4 $. Notice also that the sidelength $\\ell(Q_4)$ of each $Q_4$ is $n/M_4$.\nL173: Now partition the torus into $M_5^2$ equal squares $Q_5$ and consider shifts by multiples of $\\varepsilon_5 n/M_5$. Fix one square $Q_4\\in P_4$ and choose $N_5=\\frac{2\\log_2 (M_5/\\varepsilon_5)}{\\varepsilon_5}=2\\cdot(5^3+5)\\cdot 2^5$ independent random squares in it creating some configuration $P'_5$. Repeat the same configuration in all other squares $Q_4$ to get a configuration $P_5$ of $N_4N_5$ squares $Q_5$ with sidelength $\\ell(Q_5)=n/M_5$.\nL174: Since for all such shifts at least one square $Q_4$ in $P_4$ satisfies $d(E',Q_4)\\le\\mu_4$, the same probabilistic argument results in the conclusion that one can choose $P_5'$ so that for every shift $E'$ by multiples of $\\varepsilon_5n/M_5$ there will be a square $Q_5$ in $P_5$ with $d(E',Q_5)\\le\\mu_4+\\varepsilon_5$, which, by approximation, yields again that for every shift $E'$ we shall have some $Q_5\\in P_5$ with $d(E',Q_5)\\le\\mu_5$. The extra joining cost is now $2nN_4N_5/M_4$.\nL175: Continue the same way until we reach $P_K$ consisting of $N_4\\dots N_K$ squares $Q_K$ of sidelength $n/M_K\\le 2^{3K^2+4K+2}$. Now just fill these squares completely. This will create $$ 2^{O(K^2)}N_4\\dots N_K\\le 2^{O(K^2)}N_K^K=2^{O(K^2)}[2(K^3+K)2^K]^K=2^{O(K^2)}<Cn $$ cells out of which one is not covered for any shift of $E$.\nL176: It remains to estimate the joining cost. It is $2n$ times the series whose general term is (putting $M_3=1$) $$ \\frac{N_4\\dots N_k}{M_{k-1}}\\le\\frac{N_k^k}{M_{k-1}}=\\frac{2^{O(k^2)}}{2^{(k-1)^3}}\\, $$ so we are fine again.\nL177: \nL178: This construction is a bit cumbersome and rather unpleasant to write down (though the idea is fairly simple), so I apologize in advance for somewhat awkward exposition. As usual, feel free to ask questions if something is unclear.\nL179: \nL180: [Button: Cite]\nL181: \nL182: 【33†Improve this answer】\nL183: Follow [Input]\nL184: \nL185: answered May 24, 2022 at 4:31\nL186: \nL187: 【34†fedja】\nL188: \nL189: 64.7k 11 11 gold badges 170 170 silver badges 313 313 bronze badges\nL190:   * Could you spell out why it suffices to find a connected set of size $Cn$ which is not contained in any shift of $E$? I figured this is supposed to prove that the optimal shape inside $Cn \\times Cn$ has to have large density somewhere. I see that you are individually cheating the boundedly many subrectangles, but what if the $P$ that cheats one subrectangle is covered by another?\nL191: \nL192: –  【35†Ville Salo】\nL193: \nL194: Commented May 24, 2022 at 7:30\nL195:   * @VilleSalo Suppose that we have any set $E_0$ on the plane whose shifts can cover any $Cn$-mino on the plane. Then $E$ that is obtained by wrapping $E_0$ to the $n\\times n$ torus should have the property that its shifts on the torus cover any $Cn$-mino contained in an $n\\times n$ square placed on the torus. But we have proved that it is impossible if $E$ has fewer than $n^2/2$ cells and, obviously, $|E_0|\\ge |E|$.\nL196: \nL197: –  【34†fedja】\nL198: \nL199: Commented May 24, 2022 at 11:06\nL200:   * @VilleSalo As to the second question, $P_K$ only has the property that every shift $E'$ of $E$ cannot fill at least one of the squares $Q_K$ but which one depends on the shift. Or, perhaps, I misunderstand what you are asking there?\nL201: \nL202: –  【34†fedja】\nL203: \nL204: Commented May 24, 2022 at 11:11\nL205: \nL206:   * Oh, duh. I guess in terms of how I imagined this, the short explanation is you do not cheat the finitely many subrectangles separately, you cheat their union.\nL207: \nL208: –  【35†Ville Salo】\nL209: \nL210: Commented May 24, 2022 at 13:17\nL211:   * I am very late in reacting to this, but thanks for your answer. I did not redo the calculations but I think I see the overall idea. It is a very nice argument, and very striking that this can be shown to be $\\Theta(n^2)$. Thanks a lot for your work!\nL212: \nL213: –  【24†a3nm】\nL214: \nL215: Commented Nov 3, 2022 at 20:23\nL216: \nL217: 【32†Add a comment】  |\nL218: \nL219: [Input]\nL220: \nL221: 6\nL222: \nL223: [Input]\nL224: \nL225: It seems $S_n$ is $\\geq\\displaystyle\\Theta\\left(\\frac{n^2}{\\log(n)}\\right)$.\nL226: In the following, I will consider polyominos as subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}^2$ (so, a polyomino is represented by the set of centers of its squares). Thus two polyominos which are translates of each other will be considered different.\nL227: Fix $n$ and let $P$ be a set of $n$-polyominos which contain the point $0\\in\\mathbb{Z}^2$ (we will specify $P$ later). For any $X\\subseteq\\mathbb{Z}^2$, we define $P_X=\\{p\\in P;p\\subseteq X\\}$. Then the set of polyominos of $P$ such that some translate of them is contained in $X$ will be $\\bigcup_{x\\in X}P_{X-x}$.\nL228: If $A\\subset\\mathbb{Z}^2$ is a set which contains some translate of all polyominos of $P$, then $P=\\bigcup_{a\\in A}P_{A-a}$. So for some $a\\in A$, $\\#P_{A-a}\\geq\\frac{\\#P}{\\#A}$. So if we want $A$ to have few elements, $P_{A-a}$ will contain a lot of polyominos. This in turn can be used to obtain a lower bound for $\\#A$.\nL229: \nL230: Now let's define our specific choice of the set $P$.\nL231: Let $B=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{Z}^2;x,y\\text{ are even};|x|,|y|<\\frac{n}{20\\sqrt{\\log(n)}}\\}$, so $\\#B=\\left(1+2\\lfloor\\frac{n}{40\\sqrt{\\log(n)}}\\rfloor\\right)^2$.\nL232: \nL233: We will need a lemma:\nL234: \nL235: Lemma: Given $l$ points $p_i=(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^l$ contained in a square $Q$ of side $k$, there is a polyomino of length $<10k\\sqrt{l}$ containing all the points $p_i$.\nL236: Proof: The statement is true if $l=1$, so we can use induction on $l$. If we have $l+1$ points inside $Q$, then two of them, which we call $p_0,p_1$, must be at distance $<\\frac{3k}{\\sqrt{l}}$: if not, the $L_1$ balls of center $p_i$ and radius $\\frac{3k}{2\\sqrt{l}}$ would be disjoint, so as each ball intersects $Q$ in at least a quarter of its area, the area of $Q$ would be $\\geq l\\cdot\\frac{9k^2}{8l}>k^2$, a contradiction.\nL237: So we can join the points $p_0,p_1$ using a polyomino of $<4\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{l}}$ squares, and now we use that $10k\\sqrt{l}+4\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{l}}<10k\\sqrt{l+1}.\\square$\nL238: Now suppose we have a subset $C$ of $B$ with $\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor$ elements. As in the lemma above, we can choose a $n$-polyomino $p_C$ with $p_C\\cap B=C$: the proof is the same as the proof of the lemma except that we have to make sure the polyomino joining $p_0$ to $p_1$ is disjoint from $B$ except in the ends. This adds at most $2$ squares to the polyomino, so the bounds from the lemma still work.\nL239: We will let $P=\\{p_C;C\\subseteq B,\\# C=\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor\\}$, so $\\#P=\\binom{\\#B}{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}$.\nL240: Now suppose $A\\subseteq\\mathbb{Z}^2$ contains translates of all the polyominos of $P$ and $\\#A<\\frac{n^2}{10^{10}\\log(n)}$. Then, for some $a\\in A$ we have $\\#P_{A-a}\\geq\\frac{\\#P}{n^2}$. But on the other hand, $\\#(A-a)\\cap B\\leq\\#A<\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor$. Thus $P_{A-a}$ has $\\binom{\\#((A-a)\\cap B)}{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}<\\binom{\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor}{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}$ elements.\nL241: So $\\frac{\\#P_{A-a}}{\\#P}<\\frac{\\binom{\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor}{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}}{\\binom{\\#B}{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}} = \\frac{\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor\\left(\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor-1\\right)\\dots\\left(\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor-\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor+1\\right)}{\\#B(\\#B-1)\\dots(\\#B-\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor+1))}$.\nL242: Moreover, as $\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor<\\#B$, for any $i=0,\\dots,\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor-1$ we have $\\frac{\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor-i}{\\#B-i}\\leq\\frac{\\lfloor\\frac{\\#B}{100}\\rfloor}{\\#B}\\leq\\frac{1}{100}$\nL243: \nL244: So $\\frac{\\#P_{A-a}}{\\#P}\\leq\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)^{\\lfloor\\log(n)\\rfloor}<\\frac{1}{n^2}$, a contradiction.\nL245: \nL246: Maybe a better choice of $P$ or other changes to this method could improve the bound on the asymptotic growth of $S_n$ a bit more.\nL247: \nL248: [Button: Cite]\nL249: \nL250: 【36†Improve this answer】\nL251: Follow [Input]\nL252: \nL253: 【37†edited May 6, 2022 at 18:43】\nL254: \nL255: answered Apr 26, 2022 at 22:45\nL256: \nL257: 【25†Saúl RM】\nL258: \nL259: 14.6k 2 2 gold badges 39 39 silver badges 64 64 bronze badges\n"